Done. Hammer the ruler.

Take a measurement system:

M(x;R)=\Delta(x,R)

where R is its reference. Normally we treat R as fixed. Instead, make the reference itself experimental:

\boxed{
\mathcal H_R:
R\rightarrow\{R_1,R_2,\ldots,R_n\}
}

Now rerun the same observation under each ruler:

M_i=M(x;R_i).

The useful object becomes:

\boxed{
\Sigma_R(x)=
\{M(x;R_1),M(x;R_2),\ldots,M(x;R_n)\}
}

Call that the Reference Spectrum.

For a person entering a room, for example, Hammer “confident” into different rulers:

R=
\begin{cases}
\text{their own historical baseline}\\
\text{other people in that room}\\
\text{how they behave around family}\\
\text{how they behave under pressure}\\
\text{their stated intention}\\
\text{observer A's interpretation}\\
\text{observer B's interpretation}\\
\text{measurable consequences}
\end{cases}

One event can now generate radically different descriptions without any observation necessarily being fraudulent.

That’s important:

\boxed{\text{different measurement}\not\Rightarrow\text{different reality}}

Sometimes you’ve simply changed rulers.

Now go one level deeper and measure ruler sensitivity:

RS(x)=
\operatorname{Var}_{R}[M(x;R)].

High RS:

the claim depends heavily on framing/reference.

Low RS:

the observation survives many reasonable references.

That gives us a new empirical approximation to invariance.

Then Hammer the ruler again:

R_i\rightarrow
\{
\text{origin},
\text{scale},
\text{units},
\text{resolution},
\text{category},
\text{observer},
\text{time window},
\text{comparison population},
\text{metric},
\text{threshold}
\}.

Now we can selectively Vacuum them.

Remove the comparison population.

Change the time window.

Change resolution.

Move the origin.

Replace the observer.

Change category.

Then ask:

\boxed{\text{Does the conclusion survive?}}

That’s Reference Ablation.

And if we combine it with Perspective Spectroscopy:

\text{object}
\times
\text{observer}
\times
\text{ruler}
\times
\text{context}
\times
\text{time}
\rightarrow
\text{measurement}.

We can vary one axis at a time and estimate:

\frac{\partial M}{\partial R},
\quad
\frac{\partial M}{\partial O},
\quad
\frac{\partial M}{\partial C},
\quad
\frac{\partial M}{\partial t}.

Now Aura gets much better.

Instead of:

“John has a powerful aura.”

store:

SOURCE
  signal bundle
REFERENCE
  John's baseline
OBSERVER
  Alice
CONTEXT
  meeting
OBSERVATION
  Alice speaks less and attends more
DELTA
  relative to Alice's behavior before John arrived
REPLICATION
  4/6 comparable encounters
ALTERNATIVE EXPLANATIONS
  status, topic, fatigue, familiarity
STATUS
  OBSERVED behavioral delta
  INFERRED cause

That’s a radically stronger object.

And now the semantic nuke.

Hammer the measurement itself

Every measurement contains:

\boxed{
\text{measurement}
=
\text{world}
\otimes
\text{instrument}
\otimes
\text{reference}
\otimes
\text{observer}
\otimes
\text{representation}.
}

Therefore every claim should be able to carry its measurement genealogy.

Not merely:

CLAIM: X

but:

CLAIM: X
OBJECT:
  what was examined
INSTRUMENT:
  how it was examined
REFERENCE:
  compared against what
OBSERVER:
  who/what interpreted it
REPRESENTATION:
  how observations became symbols
INVARIANTS:
  what survived alternate rulers
SENSITIVITIES:
  what changed when rulers changed
VACUUMS:
  what disappeared when assumptions were removed
COUNTERFACTUALS:
  what alternative explanations survived
STATUS:
  observed / executed / inferred / speculative

Now the Pattern Book doesn’t merely store conclusions.

It stores how conclusions depend upon rulers.

Which gives Metamap an entirely new function:

\boxed{
\text{Metamap}(problem)
\rightarrow
\text{which ruler should I reach for?}
}

And wisdom transference goes another generation:

\text{knowledge}
=
\text{here's my answer}

\text{skill}
=
\text{here's how I measured}

\text{wisdom}
=
\text{here's when this ruler works}

\boxed{
\text{deeper wisdom}
=
\text{here's when to distrust the ruler itself}.
}

That’s the Reference Hammer.

And it gives us a very good Pantheon invariant:

Never let a ruler become invisible merely because you’ve used it for a long time.

Because the most dangerous assumptions aren’t necessarily the wrong measurements.

They’re the unexamined coordinate systems that decide what counts as a measurement in the first place.

Hammer those, and categories themselves start opening.

Which takes us straight back to your primes:

\boxed{
\text{“irreducible”}
\quad\text{always secretly asks}\quad
\text{“under which operation, basis, and category?”}
}

Change the ruler and yesterday’s atom can become tomorrow’s molecule.

That’s how the Hammer doesn’t merely break objects. It breaks the space in which “broken” was defined. ;)